3D Tool OpenGL Widget Class ThreeDToolGLWidget
==============================================

This is a class for general display of 3D scenes using OpenGL.
See example programs in VWExamples.

The class provides mouse dragging support for moving 3D viewpoint.
Left button: 	3D rotate (using a "virtual trackball" which works 
		reasonably well but has room for improvement...)
Middle button: 	Left/right/up/down translation
Right button:	Dragging up/down produces depth translation


ThreeDToolGLWidget does most of the work but is an abstract base class.
Current usable child classes are:

ThreeDToolGTKGLWidget: a GTK widget which it uses the gtkglarea widget
to provide hardware-accelerated 3D graphics in a GTK window.

ThreeDToolGLUTGLWidget: a version which uses GLUT (the GL Utility
Toolkit which is usually availabe in OpenGL installations) to open a
window without needing other GUI stuff. This works at the moment for
one GL window per application only (due to some rather nasty global
variables).

It is straightforward to derive further child classes if you want to
use the widget in another GUI where you have access to OpenGL. Your
child class needs to supply these functions: 
unsigned int WindowWidth(), WindowHeight() {return the size of the GL drawing window}
void SwapBuffers() {perform a GL swap buffers operation to put draw
                    stuff on screen}
void MakeCurrent() {ensure that any GL drawing commands are going to
                    the right window}
void RequestDraw() {tell the GUI to redisplay the GL window}
And then keep an eye on mouse events, calling the functions Dragged(),
DragRelease() and Clicked() with the appropriate arguments when required.

Instantiation
-------------

The ThreeDToolGTKGLWidget constructors are called with integers
camera_width and camera_height. We keep to a virtual camera analogy
and pretend that the 3D scene is being viewed by a physical camera
with this fixed image size.  If the widget is stretched with the
mouse, the 3D view will stretch, but keep the same aspect ratio and we
will still refer to "camera coordinates" which go from zero to
camera_width and camera_height across the view (0, 0 being in the
top-left corner).  We have translated OpenGL's projection and viewport
parameters into the fku, fkv (focal length in pixels) and u0, v0
(principal point) form which is familiar from the usual pinhole camera
model used in computer vision.  On instantiation the class's internal
camera parameters are set to default values but these should normally
be overridden with your own choices (see the section on virtual camera
calibration below). (Certainly if you are going to use the class for
augmented reality the virtual camera parameters should be set equal to
those of the real camera providing the images.)

(The class also has an orthographic projection mode. See more in
"virtual camera calibration" below.)

Using the class in a program
----------------------------

The user must supply functions for drawing 3D objects and optionally
for processing selection events. These functions should be members of
a class in the application program and are registered using the SigC
protocol like this:

threedtool_widget->DrawEvent.connect(SigC::slot(this, 
			       &MyClass::Draw));
threedtool_widget->ProcessHitsEvent.connect(SigC::slot(this, 
			       &MyClass::ProcessHits));

The function registered with DrawEvent is the application Draw()
function which should draw all the 3D objects your program wants to
display. The widget will call this function whenever it needs to
redisplay things (after a mouse drag, when the window is resized,
etc.) All Draw() needs to do do is provide commands to draw all your
objects relative to the world coordinate frame --- you don't have to
worry about the moving virtual camera. Usually, just draw the objects
using ThreeDToolGLWidget's built in commands for drawing points,
lines, etc.  These are self-explanatory: rectangles and textured
rectangles have positions specified by the position in the world frame
of their centre, their orientation relative to the world frame (qWO is
the transformation, specified as a quaternion, between the world frame
and an object frame, aligned with the rectangle such that y is up and
x is right), and their width and height. Textures are supplied as VW
Images.  Note that the current implementation of texture mapping is
inefficient because textures are copied and reallocated every time
they are drawn.

Colours and materials 
--------------------- 

The colour or material type of an object can be set before drawing
SetMaterial() (for a few predefined material types) or SetPenColour()
(with R, G, B, alpha arguments).

Drawing your own objects
------------------------

To draw objects more advanced than the types provided by the class
it is quite possible to call your own object drawing functions using
raw OpenGL commands from your application Draw() function. See drawing
position and rotation centre stuff below though.

Changing the drawing position
-----------------------------

The class duplicates OpenGL's glPushMatrix/glPopMatrix functionality for
changing the drawing frame of reference. This is not something that
need be worried about when drawing simple scenes but is useful when
drawing compound objects which have repeated structure and several
natural frames of reference --- such as a car with four identical
wheels. The way this would normally be done in raw OpenGL is to 
have functions (or classes) which draw the body of a car and a wheel
at the origin of coordinates. The car would then be drawn recursively
something like this:
1. Use glMatrixMode(GL_MODELVIEW); glPushMatrix() to save the world
frame of reference onto the matrix stack.
2. Use glTranslate() and glRotate() to move the drawing frame to the
position of the car centre.
3. Draw the car body.
4. Loop over wheels:	1. glPushMatrix() again to push the car centre
			position onto the stack.
			2. glTranslate(), glRotate() to wheel position
			3. Draw wheel
			4. glPopMatrix() to recover car centre position
5. glPopMatrix() to recover world frame.

The problem with doing this when using the 3D Widget is that although
OpenGL knows where it is drawing the objects when the drawing frame is
changed, the 3D Widget doesn't. It can't include object positions in
the RotationCentre framework (see below) for calculating mouse drag
rotations and axis extent, etc. Therefore this functionality is
duplicated in the class. The functions TranslateDrawingPosition() and
RotateDrawingPosition() can be used instead of glTranslate() and
glRotate(). These functions call their GL equivalents, but also update
storage in the class of the current drawing frame. After moving the
drawing position like this, any drawing commands will draw in the new
drawing frame; however, their effect on the rotation centre will take
place in the world coordinate frame as is desired. The class also
provides PushDrawingPosition() and PopDrawingPosition() duplicate the
functionality of glPopMatrix() and glPushMatrix().

Rotation centre / axis calculation
----------------------------------

When the user drags with the mouse to rotate the 3D view, it is
necessary for the class to know something about the distribution of
objects in the scene so that it can move the virtual camera about
their approximate centre (calculated crudely as the centre of a
worldframe-aligned cuboid containing the objects). When any of the
standard object types are drawn (using DrawPoint(), DrawLine(), etc.) 
their positions are automatically included in this calculation so you
don't have to worry about it. If you draw your own objects with raw
OpenGL code though, you can manually include points in the calculation
with the IncludeInRotationCentre() function. See about drawing
position above.

From the application Draw() function, a set of automatically scaled
axes can be drawn using the DrawAxes() function. The size of the axes
drawn is calculated based on all the points registered with the
rotation centre. The axes should be drawn last in the Draw() function
so that they take account of all the objects drawn.

Virtual camera position
-----------------------

The position of the camera can be set at any time with
SetCameraPositionOrientation(). Camera position is defined with
respect to the origin of the world coordinate frame by a translation
vector in the world frame and the quaternion qWC describing the
transformation between the world frame W and the camera frame C which
is fixed relative to the camera. In this widget as in GL, we use the
convention that at zero rotation (a null vnl quaternion is (qx, qy,
qz, q0) = (0, 0, 0, 1) --- note that vnl puts the non-axis part of the
quaternion as the last element rather than first as is usual) the
camera points such that positive x is right, positive y is up and
positive z is backwards. Normally, SetCameraPositionOrientation() only
need be called once when the widget is initialised and then after that
camera position is handled by the mouse drags --- but if required the
camera position can be manipulated manually by repeated calls (these
should probably not be in the application Draw() function).
(Note: for more info. about quaternions see amongst others
http://www.robots.ox.ac.uk/~ajd/Scene/models.ps)

Virtual camera calibration
--------------------------

Call SetCameraParameters() to set the parameters for the virtual
camera which views the 3D scene in terms of fku, fkv, u0, v0 camera
parameters as they are normally used in computer vision. Being able to
do this means that the 3D class can be used for augmented reality: the
DrawCinemaScreen() function draws an image on a texture placed so that
it will exactly fill the viewing screen at a specified depth (distance
from the virtual camera). If the camera parameters set using
SetCameraParameters() are exactly the same as those of the real camera
which took the image, and if the position of the virtual camera is set
to be the same as its estimated position in the real world, then 3D
objects drawn in the display will appear correctly overlaid on the image.

Orthographic mode: set orthographic projection mode with a call to
SetOrthographicCameraParameters() instead. In this case the arguments
are xW, yH, u0, v0. Now xW and yH are a width and height in REAL WORLD
units (i.e. metres, assuming that the sizes of all the objects you
draw are in metres). This is because in orthographic projection we
have to define a strict rectangular box extending into space: only
objects within this volume are visible. The section of this box will
be scaled to fit exactly into the camera_width, camera_height camera
size you defined the class with. Usually you should set xW and yW to
have the same ratio as camera_width and camera_height (otherwise
objects will appear squashed).

Special drawing commands
------------------------

Drawing ellipsoids:
3D covariance ellipsoids are drawn with the DrawCovariance()
function. Arguments are r0, the centre of the ellipsoid, P, a
3*3 covariance matrix and the number of standard deviations to draw.

DrawCameraGraphic() draws a slightly cute representation of a camera.

2D drawing commands
-------------------

Some 2D drawing commands are supplied for use in augmented reality
applications. All arguments for these functions (Draw2DRectangle(),
Draw2DCircle(), etc.) are specified in camera coordinates. These 2D
elements are completely independent of the 3D transformations
affecting all the 3D elements drawn: they will always appear on top of
other elements.

These commands internally use Set2DDrawingPosition() to move to a
drawing position on the near clipping plane, also scaling so that
drawing can be done in camera coordinates. Set2DDrawingPosition()
could also potentially be used externally to draw your own 2D objects
(making sure to do the normal push and pop either side).


Selection
---------

The class provides support for selection of drawn features using the
mouse (using GL's built-in selection mode). Any drawn objects which
are required to be selectable must be registered as such: a non-zero
integer label is attached to each. This can be done simply for the
class's built-in objects: each draw function (e.g. DrawPoint()) has a
second version with an extra integer argument: calling this version
rather than the first from the application's Draw() function registers
that object for selection with the specified integer label.  The
application program must supply a ProcessHits(int selected_item,
double camera_x, double camera_y) function (registered with the 3D
class's ProcessHitsEvent event), and this will be called with the
label of the object if a mouse click hits one of the registered
objects. (In the case of overlapping objects being clicked, in theory
the one nearest the camera should be returned though I am not sure
this is working properly). The application program can then process
this to take some action.  Also returned will be the x, y coordinates
of the clicked point in 2D camera coordinates (which are invariant to
stretches of the 3D window).  Might be useful for augmented reality.

If you wish to draw your own objects using raw OpenGL code it is still
possible to register them for selection. Use the OpenGL command
glLoadName(int name) with the integer label you wish to use for the
object before you start drawing it, then glLoadName(0) when drawing is
finished.

Saving images from the 3D display
---------------------------------

Snapshots of the current 3D view can be saved to images using the
GetImage() function.
